# ord-setting-v1 — attacking a certified comparison, for worked example S4
#
# S2 shows the ord@1 layering with nothing attacked. S4 attacks it, and this
# policy declares the vocabulary that makes the attack land where it should.
#
# The design claim under test is the factivity firewall: an accepted
# certificate discharges the ARITHMETIC and nothing else. It never asserts that
# a premise cell is true, and it never asserts that the two cells are
# comparable. So a critic who disputes the comparison cannot touch the strict
# step — there is nothing there to dispute, 0.71 < 0.74 either holds or it does
# not — and must instead attack the defeasible layer. This policy gives that
# critic exactly one place to stand: the binding.
#
# Rules:
#
#   * beats_recheck — STRICT, identical to ord-v1's. Certified by ord@1.
#   * beats_baseline — DEFEASIBLE bridge, identical to ord-v1's.
#   * setting_audit — DEFEASIBLE. An audit finding that the two cells were
#     produced under different evaluation settings. Its conclusion is declared
#     contrary to comparison_setup, so an instance UNDERMINES the binding leaf.

policy ord-setting-v1

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2:
# every predicate and constructor symbol this policy's rules and programs may use, with the sort
# of each argument position. `Num` and `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric
# and string literals) and are not declarable.
#
# Generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` (union-find over argument occurrences, finest consistent
# partition), then the sorts were given their intended names — the one editorial step, and the
# only one:
#
#   cabal exec -- runghc --ghc-arg=-package --ghc-arg=lara scripts/infer-sigma.hs \
#     examples/S4/ord-setting-v1.policy.lara examples/S4/example.lara

sort Experiment, Cell, System, Measurand, Dataset, Auditor

con accuracy : Measurand
con aud1 : Auditor
con exp1 : Experiment
con imagenet_val : Dataset
con score_cell(System, Measurand, Dataset, Num) : Cell
con sys_base : System
con sys_new : System

pred audits_settings(Auditor, System, System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred better(System, System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred comparison_setup(System, System, Measurand, Dataset, Num, Num)
pred num_lt(Num, Num)
pred reports(Experiment, Cell)
pred settings_differ(System, System, Measurand, Dataset, Num, Num)

# --- The strict leg (ord-v1's beats_recheck, unchanged) -------------------------------------

rule beats_recheck(S, B, Q, D, Exp, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, score_cell(B, Q, D, Bv)),
                 reports(Exp, score_cell(S, Q, D, Sv)) ]
  conclusion = num_lt(Bv, Sv)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (ord@1, sha256:ord-setting-v1-theory-0) ]

theory sha256:ord-setting-v1-theory-0 = []

# --- The bridge (ord-v1's beats_baseline, unchanged) ----------------------------------------
# The premise labels (lara-syntax@0.3, App. B.4) are what make S4's attack readable. Slot 1 is
# the binding, and `binding` names it, so example.lara writes `undermine x1 a2.binding.leaf`.
# The label is resolved here, against this rule, at elaboration time; `a2.1.leaf` remains legal
# and means exactly the same thing. Labels never enter the Unit — both spellings compile to the
# same Undermine at the same premise — and each round-trips in the spelling it was written in.
#
# The disambiguation is a policy property, not a parse heuristic: a label may not collide with
# one of this rule's question ids and may not be spelled `rule` or `leaf`, both checked when the
# policy is declared, so `a2.binding.leaf` can be read exactly one way.

rule beats_baseline(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ cmp:     num_lt(Bv, Sv),
                 binding: comparison_setup(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv) ]
  conclusion = better(S, B, Q, D)

# --- The critic: the two numbers are not comparable -----------------------------------------
# An audit that finds the two cells were measured under different settings — different input
# resolution, different decoding budget, different test split. The numbers are each perfectly
# real; what fails is that they answer the same question.
#
# Sv and Bv appear in the conclusion but not in the premise. That is deliberate and it is legal:
# a rule instance supplies its whole substitution explicitly, so the audit names the two values
# whose pairing it is disputing. Without them the conclusion could not be contrary to a
# comparison_setup atom, which carries the values.

rule setting_audit(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv, Aud)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ audits_settings(Aud, S, B, Q, D) ]
  conclusion = settings_differ(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv)

# --- Conflict --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE direction only: an audit finding defeats a binding, and nothing here licenses a binding to
# attack the audit back. A binding is an author's attestation that two numbers are comparable;
# restating it is not evidence against an audit that examined the settings. The asymmetry is the
# modeling claim, and the grounded outcome below depends on it — a symmetric pair would make the
# two contested instead, which is E5's shape, not this one.

contrary settings_differ(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv) comparison_setup(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv)

# --- The @0.3 comparison surface ------------------------------------------------------------
# ord-v1's declarations, unchanged, so that S4's artifact can be S2's artifact plus an audit.
# The scheme is what makes the generated bridge attackable in the first place: the elaborator
# emits an argument by the named defeasible rule, so it has the same premise structure — and the
# same attack surface — as the hand-written one it replaced. Sugar generates the argument; it
# does not protect it.

measurand accuracy : Num where higher-is-better

comparison-scheme strictly-better higher-is-better
  recheck = beats_recheck
  bridge  = beats_baseline

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): the only strict rule is beats_recheck, whose conclusion
# pattern num_lt(Bv, Sv) heads no contrary pair. The declared pair is on comparison_setup /
# settings_differ, and neither is the conclusion of a strict rule, so no contrary side is
# strict-reachable and the policy passes the R12 check.
#
# This is the structural reason the attack cannot reach the arithmetic. To rebut the strict step
# a critic would need a contrary of num_lt — and by the intra-family exclusion lemmas in
# lean/Lara/Ord.lean no sound backend can ever certify one over the same literals, so declaring
# it would be both useless and an R12 violation. The comparison is settled; only its relevance
# is arguable.
